Binary Tree:
---------------

-   A Tree is a hierarchical data structure that is different from linear data structures like arrays, linked lists, stacks,
    and queues
-   The root node of a Binary Tree is the top-most node from where the tree hierarchy begins.
-   Leaf nodes are those nodes that lie at the terminal part of the tree indicating the last or bottom-most part of the
    tree.
-   Children of a node are the nodes to which the pointers from the node directly point.
-   The ancestors of a given node are all the nodes that come before the node while traversing from the root node to
    the given node.
-   A Binary Tree is a type of tree in which each node cannot have more than 2 children nodes.
-   Types of Binary tree:
    -   Full Binary tree:each node has either 0 or 2 children.
    -   Complete Binary tree: All levels are completely filled, The last level must have each node as left as possible.
    -   Perfect Binary Tree – It is a type of tree in which all the leaf nodes should be on the same level.
    -   Balanced Binary Tree – In a balanced binary tree, the height of the tree should be at max log(N), where N is
        the number of nodes. The tree should not be skewed

